    "heel": {
      "word": "HEEL",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "To lean or tip to one side, as a ship; as, the ship heels aport; the boat heeled over when the squall struck it. Heeling error (Naut.), a deviation of the compass caused by the heeling of an iron vessel to one side or the other.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "The hinder part of the foot; sometimes, the whole foot; -- in man or quadrupeds. He [the stag] calls to mind his strength and then his speed, His winged heels and then his armed head. Denham.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The hinder part of any covering for the foot, as of a shoe, sock, etc.; specif., a solid part projecting downward from the hinder part of the sole of a boot or shoe.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The latter or remaining part of anything; the closing or concluding part. \"The heel of a hunt.\" A. Trollope. \"The heel of the white loaf.\" Sir W. Scott.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Anything regarded as like a human heel in shape; a protuberance; a knob.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "The part of a thing corresponding in position to the human heel; the lower part, or part on which a thing rests; especially: (a) (Naut.) The after end of a ship's keel. (b) (Naut.) The lower end of a mast, a boom, the bowsprit, the sternpost, etc. (c) (Mil.) In a small arm, the corner of the but which is upwards in the firing position. (d) (Mil.) The uppermost part of the blade of a sword, next to the hilt. (e) The part of any tool next the tang or handle; as, the heel of a scythe.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Man.)",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "Management by the heel, especially the spurred heel; as, the horse understands the heel well.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "(Arch.) (a) The lower end of a timber in a frame, as a post or rafter. In the United States, specif., the obtuse angle of the lower end of a rafter set sloping. (b) A cyma reversa; -- so called by workmen. Gwilt. Heel chain (Naut.), a chain passing from the bowsprit cap around the heel of the jib boom. of, to pursue closely; to follow hard: as, hungry want is at my heels. Otway. hence, to be shabby, or in a poor plight. Shak. uppermost; hence, to move in a inconsiderate, or rash, manner. Addison.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To perform by the use of the heels, as in dancing, running, and the like. [R.] I cannot sing, Nor heel the high lavolt. Shak.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To add a heel to; as, to heel a shoe.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        },
        {
          "text": "To arm with a gaff, as a cock for fighting.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
